**Summary of evidence:**
- srv1 (10.5.0.4) is the campus DNS server — `named` is running (PID 29), listening on TCP+UDP port 53, no firewall blocks
- Zone file `/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab` has **only a wildcard record** `* IN A 10.5.0.4` — so every campus FQDN (pc1.jeremysitlab.com, pc3.jeremysitlab.com, etc.) resolves to 10.5.0.4 (the DNS server's own IP), not the host's real address
- All clients (pc1, pc2, pc3) have only `nameserver 10.5.0.4` in resolv.conf — **no `search jeremysitlab.com`** — so bare hostname queries (`ping pc1`) are sent as `pc1.` (root zone), which the server refuses (`REFUSED`) because `recursion no` and it has no root zone
- `pc3.jeremysitlab.com` → 10.5.0.4 confirmed wrong; `pc1.jeremysitlab.com` → 10.5.0.4 confirmed wrong
- L3 paths are fully functional (Phase 1: 0% loss everywhere)

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The campus DNS server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) is running and reachable, but its BIND zone file contains a wildcard catch-all record (`* IN A 10.5.0.4`) instead of individual A records for each campus host. This causes every hostname query under `jeremysitlab.com` to resolve to 10.5.0.4 — the DNS server's own address — rather than the target host's actual IP. Simultaneously, the server is configured with `recursion no`, meaning it will only answer queries for names it is authoritative over, and all three client hosts have only `nameserver 10.5.0.4` in `/etc/resolv.conf` with no `search jeremysitlab.com` suffix. When users try bare hostnames like `ping pc1`, the resolver queries `pc1.` (the root), which named refuses (`REFUSED`, "not found"). If users try FQDNs like `pc1.jeremysitlab.com`, the wildcard returns 10.5.0.4 instead of pc1's real IP (10.1.0.100), causing connections to reach the DNS server itself rather than the intended host. IP-level connectivity across the campus fabric is fully healthy; only name resolution is broken. The root cause is the incorrect wildcard zone record in `/etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab` on srv1. The fix is to replace the `* IN A 10.5.0.4` wildcard with explicit, correct A records for every campus host (pc1 → 10.1.0.100, pc2 → 10.1.0.101, pc3 → 10.3.0.100, etc.) and add `search jeremysitlab.com` to all clients' `/etc/resolv.conf`.
=== END ===